<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Caxcanes</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Caxcan people</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Caxcanes were an indigenous people of what are now the Mexican states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zacatecas">Zacatecas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalisco">Jalisco</a>, in the region the Spaniards called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-galicia">New Galicia</a>. They led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mixton-war">Mixtón War</a> of 1540–1542, the largest rising against Spanish rule in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, which Viceroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-de-mendoza">Antonio de Mendoza</a> crushed with a large army of indigenous allies. Their lands were later absorbed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>-mining frontier.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/caxcanes.jpg" alt="Caxcanes" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guerra_de_juchipila.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Caxcan are indigenous to western and north-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, in lands that correspond to present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zacatecas">Zacatecas</a>, southern Durango, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalisco">Jalisco</a>, Colima, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aguascalientes">Aguascalientes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayarit">Nayarit</a>. Their language, most often described as an ancient variant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a>, belongs to the Uto-Aztecan family. Partly nomadic and partly settled, they lived in several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> with elected rulers called tlatoani, and their main religious and population centres were Teul, Tlaltenango, Juchipila and Teocaltiche.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the Mixtón Rebellion of 1540–42 the Caxcan, under their leader Tenamaztle, were allied with the Zacatecos against the Spaniards and were described as the heart and centre of the rising. Their later ceasefire with the Spaniards made them a constant target of the Zacatecos and Guachichiles. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichimeca-war">Chichimeca War</a> of 1550–1590, the longest and most expensive conflict between Spaniards and indigenous peoples in the history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>, they fought on the Spanish side against a confederation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichimeca">Chichimeca</a> peoples.</p><p>Warfare, disease and marriage with outsiders wore away much of Caxcan culture. During the colonial period many Spanish and some Basque settlers married or had relations with Caxcanes, so that many of their descendants were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mestizo">mestizos</a>, and allied peoples and mestizos settled the Caxcan lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zacatecas">Zacatecas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalisco">Jalisco</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>According to the anthropologist Aleš Hrdlička, the last generation of native speakers of Caxcan came to an end in the 1890s. Anthropologists long took this to mean the end of the Caxcan people themselves, but much of their culture has persisted through oral tradition. In the 1920s Juana Belén Gutiérrez de Mendoza, a Caxcan from Durango, formed the Council of the Caxcan indigenous people and published Alto!, a book that stressed Mexican nationalism through indigenous roots.</p><p>Caxcan people live in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zacatecas">Zacatecas</a> and in diaspora, and the Mexican government officially recognises four communities in southern Zacatecas as Caxcan. They have sought to regain legal access to their sacred mountain, Tlachialoyantepec (El Cerro de las Ventanas), and a revival of Caxcan language, scholarship and culture has been under way.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Caxcan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Mixtón War of 1540–1542 the <b>Caxcanes</b> and other peoples of New Galicia rose against Spanish rule, and Viceroy Mendoza himself led the army that crushed them with thousands of indigenous auxiliaries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Military</a></li></ul></section>
